#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "ef4a57fd-8ade-5b7d-9d6e-0fabd228e603" :question/math-academy-id "e-3546" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$. If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x &= -\\sin x \\\\\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x + \\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\sqrt{2}\\cos x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ are $x = 0,\\, \\pi$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0$, or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "4c5e27aa-f9a5-54c0-a15c-14b923eec893" :question/math-academy-id "e-3548" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta}\\tan{\\theta}-\\sin{\\theta} = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$. If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3$?" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nso these values cannot be solutions to the equation.\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3}\\sin θ\\tan θ - \\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin θ(\\sqrt{3}\\tan θ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0$, or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot 180 = 5\\,400\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "362274e9-6980-5e3b-896b-db42cfc41a9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3550" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0\n$$\n\nOn the interval\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nthe excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 0^\\circ, 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn the given interval, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x\\tan x &= \\tan x \\\\\n\\csc x\\tan x - \\tan x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\csc x - 1)\\tan x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0$, or equivalently, $\\csc x=1$. The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ$.\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dea3f3b-5c9b-5005-996a-792a6902fcce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Some Coordinates on a Graph by Solving a Trigonometric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "0aff48c8-380d-504b-a044-8ba08a2617a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3551" :question/problem "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$. The curves intersect at the points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives\nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\cos x(2\\sin x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0$, or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e58287a-4ed3-5565-8b30-1d607c85dbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107764} {:answer/id #uuid "a752ec47-dda1-5ea2-82d3-2c8db9f522ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107765} {:answer/id #uuid "b481fd2d-1f2c-509a-a639-394f0543f57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107766} {:answer/id #uuid "be5839e3-e77c-5453-893e-bba6d562a31f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107767} {:answer/id #uuid "6afda1d5-bf04-5849-acce-30e908b96c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107768}] :answer-field/id #uuid "039d55a3-f5ec-5e3a-b6e9-2733fee9f9fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107763}] :question/id #uuid "e42167e1-42b5-5316-b2f2-add419b65958" :question/math-academy-id "q-24002" :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\sin x\\cos x =-\\sin x$ for $- π \\le x < π$. If the solutions $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a2d678-ca5a-5292-b468-14a52e3572f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108618} {:answer/id #uuid "e447c647-cc52-5cc5-ba96-a27ba50f45d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108619} {:answer/id #uuid "f2342dd8-6ede-5787-b191-4571cfbcb0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108620} {:answer/id #uuid "125b0884-2248-5f55-a7e9-068076603ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108621} {:answer/id #uuid "57309f16-2aa5-51d5-8971-b6d997ad293d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108622}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5b13f2ce-c29b-57af-9ebe-adda59545e9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108617}] :question/id #uuid "59756d3c-3c85-5289-a62f-143fdaa1a9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-30184" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x\\sec x - \\sec x = 0$ for $0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
